ss", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/83": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "def starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the count of n-digit positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    Assumes n is a positive integer (n >= 1).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    # For n > 1, the count is 18 * 10^(n-2)\n    # This formula covers numbers starting with 1 (10^(n-1) possibilities)\n    # numbers ending with 1 (10^(n-1) possibilities)\n    # and subtracts numbers starting AND ending with 1 (10^(n-2) possibilities)\n    # So, 10^(n-1) + 10^(n-1) - 10^(n-2)\n    # = 2 * 10^(n-1) - 10^(n-2)\n    # = 20 * 10^(n-2) - 10^(n-2)\n    # = 19 * 10^(n-2)\n    #\n    # Wait, the design context snippet says `return 18 * (10 ** (n - 2))`\n    # Let's re-evaluate the formula provided by the design:\n    # `Behavior: If n == 1, return 1. Else return 18 * 10^(n-2).`\n    # Let's trust the design context's formula directly.\n\n    # Example: n=2. Numbers are 11, 12..19 (9 numbers starting with 1), 21, 31..91 (8 numbers ending with 1 excluding 11).\n    # 9 (starts with 1: 10-19)\n    # + 9 (ends with 1: 11, 21, ..., 91)\n    # - 1 (starts AND ends with 1: 11)\n    # = 9 + 9 - 1 = 17\n    # Design says: 18 * (10**(2-2)) = 18 * 10^0 = 18 * 1 = 18.\n    # What's the discrepancy?\n    #\n    # n-digit numbers:\n    # 1. Starts with 1: 1 followed by (n-1) digits. The first digit is 1. The remaining (n-1) digits can be anything from 0-9.\n    #    So, 1 * 10^(n-1) numbers.\n    # 2. Ends with 1: (n-1) digits followed by 1. The last digit is 1. The first digit cannot be 0.\n    #    So, 9 * 10^(n-2) * 1 numbers. (First digit 1-9, next n-2 digits 0-9, last digit 1).\n    # 3. Starts and ends with 1: 1 followed by (n-2) digits, then 1.\n    #    1 * 10^(n-2) * 1 numbers.\n    #\n    # Total = (Numbers starting with 1) + (Numbers ending with 1) - (Numbers starting AND ending with 1)\n    #\n    # For n > 1:\n    # 